d suppose $p(1)=p(2)=p(3)=0$. What can be concluded about $p$?","options":["$p$ equals $(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)$","$p$ equals $c(x-1)(x-2)$ for some $c\\ne0$","$p$ is the zero polynomial","No such polynomial $p$ exists"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ba7f27c8d48e4becc8aeaa97","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_root_of_a_polynomial_79257e06_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $p\\in\\mathbb{R}[x]$ have degree $3$, and suppose $p(1)=0$ and $p(2)=0$. Which statement about the roots of $p$ in $\\mathbb{R}$ is correct?","options":["$p$ has exactly two distinct real roots, $1$ and $2$.","$p$ has either two or three distinct real roots.","$p$ has exactly three distinct real roots.","$p$ may have a non-real root besides $1$ and $2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6a7242df8025f89ecf503408","time_limit":40}]}}